-- title:
--   The cone-restricted part of a cube is contained in the cube
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a mesh $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and a point $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let $A_h(u)$ denote the cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ (written `cube h u`). Given a configuration $\Gamma$, assigning a cone to every point of $\mathbb{R}^d$, and a set $V \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$, the *cone-restricted cube*
--   $$A_h^{V}(u) \;=\; \{\, x \in A_h(u) \;:\; V \subseteq \Gamma(x) \,\}$$
--   (written `cubeCone Γ V h u`) collects those points of the cube whose own cone contains $V$. The result is the inclusion
--   $$A_h^{V}(u) \;\subseteq\; A_h(u).$$
--
--   This is the defining containment for the cone-restricted cubes: it is the statement that the "majority" sets $A_h^m(u)$, on which the reference cone $V^m$ is available throughout, are honest subsets of the tile they refine. It is the elementary step that lets any estimate valid on a whole cube be applied to its cone-restricted part, and in particular lets Lebesgue measures of the two be compared.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis at all is imposed: $h$ may be zero or negative and $V$ may be arbitrary, in which case both sides are typically empty and the inclusion is vacuous. The statement is therefore purely a matter of the definition of `cubeCone` as a comprehension over `cube`.
